WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:00.953
- What is up?

00:00:00.953 --> 00:00:02.880
I watched my first episode of "Scrubs,"

00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:04.910
and it was a little mean-spirited for me,

00:00:04.910 --> 00:00:07.190
but you guys have been telling
me nonstop in the comments

00:00:07.190 --> 00:00:08.250
that I'm gonna love the show.

00:00:08.250 --> 00:00:09.300
I scoured the internet,

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:12.540
and found out that Brendan
Fraser is on episode 22

00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:13.373
of season one.

00:00:13.373 --> 00:00:14.206
Beeyooop!

00:00:14.206 --> 00:00:17.677
(upbeat bass heavy music)

00:00:18.721 --> 00:00:20.250
- This isn't gonna end well for you,

00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:21.850
I used to wrestle in college.

00:00:21.850 --> 00:00:23.820
- I remember one of my
favorite movies as a kid,

00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:25.790
and don't ask me why, 'cause
it's not a great movie,

00:00:25.790 --> 00:00:28.950
was with him and Elizabeth
Hurley called, "Bedazzled,"

00:00:28.950 --> 00:00:30.730
where she plays the
Devil and he gets wishes,

00:00:30.730 --> 00:00:32.080
and he gets to do all types of stuff.

00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:33.170
I loved his humor.

00:00:33.170 --> 00:00:35.490
I also found Elizabeth
Hurley very attractive.

00:00:35.490 --> 00:00:37.620
- Oh my God, that's disgusting.

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:38.900
- I already dosed him with morphine,

00:00:38.900 --> 00:00:40.640
and the x-ray says the
nail went straight through,

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:42.490
so it's not that big a...

00:00:42.490 --> 00:00:44.770
Oh, dear God, she's getting woozy.

00:00:44.770 --> 00:00:46.286
Quickly, show her the bloody side.

00:00:46.286 --> 00:00:47.119
- Look at that.

00:00:47.119 --> 00:00:48.085
- Yeah, yeah.
- Touch the nail.

00:00:48.085 --> 00:00:48.918
- Touch the nail.
- You wanna touch it?

00:00:48.918 --> 00:00:49.751
- Touch it.

00:00:49.751 --> 00:00:51.713
- Touch the nail. Touch my nail! Touch it!

00:00:51.713 --> 00:00:52.546
Lick the tip of my--

00:00:52.546 --> 00:00:53.379
(thuds)

00:00:53.379 --> 00:00:54.212
(laughs)

00:00:54.212 --> 00:00:55.045
- That's so mean,

00:00:55.045 --> 00:00:55.878
but it's true.

00:00:55.878 --> 00:00:58.870
Some people do have what we
call a vasovagal reaction

00:00:58.870 --> 00:00:59.900
when they see blood.

00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:02.640
Their blood pressure drops.
They faint temporarily.

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:04.480
This is not a deadly condition,

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:05.960
but the sight of blood, it's scary.

00:01:05.960 --> 00:01:08.860
It stimulates the part of your
nervous system that makes you

00:01:08.860 --> 00:01:09.810
fear for your life.

00:01:09.810 --> 00:01:11.840
And to some people, if
it gets overactivated,

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:13.150
you can faint like that.

00:01:13.150 --> 00:01:15.720
When I was a medical student,
I was doing a lot of ABGs,

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:17.440
which is an arterial blood gas.

00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:19.850
You have to put the needle
right into the radial artery.

00:01:19.850 --> 00:01:21.680
Unlike veins, you can't see the arteries,

00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:22.610
you have to feel them,

00:01:22.610 --> 00:01:25.060
which makes the procedure
that much more difficult,

00:01:25.060 --> 00:01:27.080
but it's a high-pressure system,

00:01:27.080 --> 00:01:29.100
so when you put the
needle in, if you miss,

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:31.900
or the needle tip is
not inserted properly,

00:01:31.900 --> 00:01:33.860
blood can start squirting out.

00:01:33.860 --> 00:01:35.850
One of my first few times doing that,

00:01:35.850 --> 00:01:38.510
I put the needle in, blood
started squirting out,

00:01:38.510 --> 00:01:39.700
the patient fainted.

00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:41.520
Luckily, they were laying in their bed,

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:44.080
but they were so worried that
they saw the sight of their

00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:45.540
own blood that they passed out.

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:47.430
- Now, let's get you to a hand surgeon.

00:01:47.430 --> 00:01:48.280
- What about tough guy?

00:01:48.280 --> 00:01:49.113
- [J.D.] Ow.

00:01:49.113 --> 00:01:49.946
- Somebody'll get her.

00:01:49.946 --> 00:01:51.240
- So, the reason why the ER doctor,

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:54.520
or the internal medicine
doctor, doesn't handle this

00:01:54.520 --> 00:01:56.960
complicated case of the nail
going through the hand is

00:01:56.960 --> 00:01:59.000
because the hand is really complicated.

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:00.980
That's why you'd need
such a subspecialist.

00:02:00.980 --> 00:02:03.690
You don't just need a surgeon,
not just a plastic surgeon,

00:02:03.690 --> 00:02:04.970
you need a hand surgeon.

00:02:04.970 --> 00:02:07.580
The hand has tendons, ligaments, bones,

00:02:07.580 --> 00:02:09.430
nerves, blood vessels,

00:02:09.430 --> 00:02:11.450
and they're all in such close proximity.

00:02:11.450 --> 00:02:13.560
If you don't know the
anatomy inside and out,

00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:16.000
and how it reacts to a
nail going through it,

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:17.400
you're gonna have a tough time.

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:19.140
There's gonna be a higher
rate of complications.

00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:19.999
- Wait, wait, wait.

00:02:19.999 --> 00:02:20.832
Stop, stop, stop.

00:02:20.832 --> 00:02:22.505
I'm guessing Mr. Weinberg is Jewish?

00:02:22.505 --> 00:02:23.850
- Yeah, so?

00:02:23.850 --> 00:02:25.249
- Why isn't he circumcised?

00:02:25.249 --> 00:02:27.410
(wacky guitar chord)

00:02:27.410 --> 00:02:28.930
- [J.D Voiceover] A recent
medical study found that a

00:02:28.930 --> 00:02:31.840
mistake is made on about
20% of all patients.

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:33.850
Most of these are clerical, and harmless,

00:02:33.850 --> 00:02:36.520
but it still adds up to
a lot of near misses.

00:02:36.520 --> 00:02:38.800
- Dr. Wen wants me to ask you
if there could be a mix-up?

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:40.830
Because our appendicitis patient...

00:02:40.830 --> 00:02:42.470
That dude doesn't have an appendix.

00:02:42.470 --> 00:02:44.194
- Hospitals make mistakes.

00:02:44.194 --> 00:02:47.420
Patients, unfortunately,
get ill as a result of

00:02:47.420 --> 00:02:48.850
a hospital complication.

00:02:48.850 --> 00:02:50.880
When you go to a hospital, if
you notice that the doctors

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:54.430
are trying to eagerly get
you out and expedited as fast

00:02:54.430 --> 00:02:55.660
as possible to go home,

00:02:55.660 --> 00:02:57.540
the reason is not because we want you out,

00:02:57.540 --> 00:03:00.210
but it's because we want to
lower the chances of you having

00:03:00.210 --> 00:03:01.400
a hospital complication.

00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:02.300
- You're pregnant.

00:03:04.026 --> 00:03:05.340
- I'm what now?

00:03:05.340 --> 00:03:08.380
- Yup! Pregnant! Your
fiance's gonna be so happy!

00:03:08.380 --> 00:03:11.110
- My fiance and I decided
not to have sex until

00:03:11.110 --> 00:03:12.630
we were married.

00:03:12.630 --> 00:03:13.860
- So, he's not gonna be so happy.

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:15.370
(laughing)
- More curious.

00:03:15.370 --> 00:03:18.540
- Can't stop thinkin' about
Mr. Weinberg's testicles.

00:03:18.540 --> 00:03:19.373
- Dude.

00:03:20.470 --> 00:03:23.800
- I mean, I almost
removed one of them, Todd.

00:03:23.800 --> 00:03:24.633
- Which one?

00:03:24.633 --> 00:03:25.466
- Like it matters.

00:03:25.466 --> 00:03:26.299
- Oh, it matters.

00:03:26.299 --> 00:03:27.290
- Fun fact about testicles:

00:03:27.290 --> 00:03:30.090
One of the testicles usually
hangs lower than the other.

00:03:30.090 --> 00:03:31.410
Some men get worried about that,

00:03:31.410 --> 00:03:33.640
and actually come see me to
make sure everything's okay,

00:03:33.640 --> 00:03:35.270
but that is a normal variant.

00:03:35.270 --> 00:03:36.550
- You're not pregnant!

00:03:36.550 --> 00:03:38.500
The lab tech just switched the samples!

00:03:40.390 --> 00:03:41.836
- You're killin' me.

00:03:41.836 --> 00:03:44.000
Hey sweetie, you know I was just jokin'

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:45.505
about all that stuff, right?

00:03:45.505 --> 00:03:47.000
- (laughs) Jesus.

00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:48.920
Samples can get switched up.

00:03:48.920 --> 00:03:50.730
This doesn't regularly happen,

00:03:50.730 --> 00:03:53.210
but we talked about this on
the past episode of "Scrubs,"

00:03:53.210 --> 00:03:56.810
that when hospitals are more
ready to admit their mistakes,

00:03:56.810 --> 00:03:59.230
and point out what they
did wrong, apologize,

00:03:59.230 --> 00:04:01.890
tell the patients what the
plan is gonna be so they don't

00:04:01.890 --> 00:04:02.840
repeat the mistake,

00:04:02.840 --> 00:04:04.760
there's less likely to get sued.

00:04:04.760 --> 00:04:06.230
That's always important to keep in mind,

00:04:06.230 --> 00:04:09.440
even though that's just a
totally hilarious scene.

00:04:09.440 --> 00:04:10.960
- So, turns out she wasn't
really pregnant after all,

00:04:10.960 --> 00:04:12.820
'cause some idiot
mislabeled her urine sample.

00:04:12.820 --> 00:04:14.840
- I was a heartbeat away from
giving an appendix patient

00:04:14.840 --> 00:04:15.872
a crotch lobotomy.

00:04:15.872 --> 00:04:16.705
- Oh my god.

00:04:16.705 --> 00:04:18.590
- If I do my best and I lose a patient,

00:04:18.590 --> 00:04:19.423
you know what?

00:04:19.423 --> 00:04:20.510
I can live with that,

00:04:20.510 --> 00:04:22.590
but if a clerical error's the
reason why a guy's walkin'

00:04:22.590 --> 00:04:24.300
around here with only the lonely...

00:04:24.300 --> 00:04:25.230
Well, damn!

00:04:25.230 --> 00:04:26.930
That don't sit well with the big dog.

00:04:26.930 --> 00:04:29.130
- Well, that's why
nowadays we do a timeout,

00:04:29.130 --> 00:04:32.340
where we literally, before
we start the operation,

00:04:32.340 --> 00:04:33.800
before the patient gets put under,

00:04:33.800 --> 00:04:37.170
we say the patient's name,
we describe the procedure.

00:04:37.170 --> 00:04:39.330
We talk about the site of the surgery.

00:04:39.330 --> 00:04:41.960
We get the nurse to confirm,
the doctor to confirm.

00:04:41.960 --> 00:04:43.380
So, everybody's on the same page,

00:04:43.380 --> 00:04:45.580
which decreases the likelihood
of making a mistake.

00:04:45.580 --> 00:04:47.380
Oh, and very important to note,

00:04:47.380 --> 00:04:49.970
while the patients asleep and
we're performing the surgery,

00:04:49.970 --> 00:04:52.740
the nurse that's in charge
of assisting the doctor is

00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:54.620
counting all of the instruments,

00:04:54.620 --> 00:04:58.330
is accounting how many
gauze pads are being used,

00:04:58.330 --> 00:04:59.620
all of the rags.

00:04:59.620 --> 00:05:03.540
Everything is counted before
and after to make sure nothing

00:05:03.540 --> 00:05:04.960
gets left inside the patient,

00:05:04.960 --> 00:05:06.860
'cause this has happened before,

00:05:06.860 --> 00:05:08.720
and it's a horrible, horrible mistake.

00:05:08.720 --> 00:05:10.540
- [J.D. Voiceover] I was paged
because Ben's blood work was

00:05:10.540 --> 00:05:11.700
finally ready.

00:05:11.700 --> 00:05:14.210
Unfortunately, the chart
wasn't in the outbox.

00:05:14.210 --> 00:05:16.850
I'd knock but the
hematopathologist is the meanest,

00:05:16.850 --> 00:05:19.250
intern-hatingest,
monster in this hospital.

00:05:19.250 --> 00:05:21.950
- It's funny because
nowadays all our results are

00:05:21.950 --> 00:05:24.100
in the computer. So, as soon
as we get results back (snaps),

00:05:24.100 --> 00:05:26.610
we just pop open a computer,
all of the results are

00:05:26.610 --> 00:05:27.690
there to review.

00:05:27.690 --> 00:05:30.100
If we have any questions, we
can contact the pathologist,

00:05:30.100 --> 00:05:32.620
or radiologist, whoever's running
the test to get more info,

00:05:32.620 --> 00:05:35.720
but computers have made
this process a lot easier.

00:05:35.720 --> 00:05:37.470
- Come on, good news.

00:05:37.470 --> 00:05:39.340
- [Carla] Hey, Bambi.

00:05:39.340 --> 00:05:40.740
- Couldn't quite catch that.

00:05:41.780 --> 00:05:42.613
- You okay?

00:05:44.120 --> 00:05:45.373
- [J.D. Voiceover] Leukemia.

00:05:46.855 --> 00:05:47.788
- No.

00:05:47.788 --> 00:05:49.710
- Leukemia's a form of blood cancer.

00:05:49.710 --> 00:05:51.930
It's been known to be a children's cancer,

00:05:51.930 --> 00:05:54.170
which is slightly incorrect
'cause more adults get it.

00:05:54.170 --> 00:05:55.610
Essentially what you have is an increase

00:05:55.610 --> 00:05:56.670
in white blood cells,

00:05:56.670 --> 00:05:58.600
and this is an improper increase,

00:05:58.600 --> 00:06:00.750
and it's such a big increase
that it pushes out the

00:06:00.750 --> 00:06:02.960
red blood cells, even the platelets.

00:06:02.960 --> 00:06:04.850
Platelets are the cells that clot.

00:06:04.850 --> 00:06:06.990
So, if you have a cut,
they're the ones that come in,

00:06:06.990 --> 00:06:08.100
and fill that clot.

00:06:08.100 --> 00:06:09.410
So, for example, here.

00:06:09.410 --> 00:06:11.810
He has such a spike in his
white blood cells that he's

00:06:11.810 --> 00:06:13.790
actually pushing out the platelets,

00:06:13.790 --> 00:06:15.090
and he's unable to clot properly.

00:06:15.090 --> 00:06:17.330
That's why he's bleeding while shaving,

00:06:17.330 --> 00:06:19.680
he's bleeding out of his wound too much,

00:06:19.680 --> 00:06:22.290
and also, something very
commonly that happens

00:06:22.290 --> 00:06:23.860
patients tells me, is when
they brush their teeth there's

00:06:23.860 --> 00:06:25.280
an excessive amount of blood,

00:06:25.280 --> 00:06:26.750
and it stays around for a while.

00:06:26.750 --> 00:06:28.669
- Oh, Janice. Are those
the test results we've

00:06:28.669 --> 00:06:30.370
been waiting for?

00:06:30.370 --> 00:06:31.203
- [J.D. Voiceover] Wait a sec...

00:06:31.203 --> 00:06:33.450
First Turk's patient, then Elliot's...

00:06:33.450 --> 00:06:35.750
This is a mistake. I
just, I feel it in my gut.

00:06:35.750 --> 00:06:36.850
I'm sure of it.

00:06:36.850 --> 00:06:38.700
- Hello? Janice?

00:06:38.700 --> 00:06:40.370
- Uh, no. This is something...

00:06:40.370 --> 00:06:43.610
You know that lab is backed up again,

00:06:43.610 --> 00:06:46.200
and so, I'm gonna, it's,
you know, a little while.

00:06:46.200 --> 00:06:48.860
- Actually, he's not wrong in doing that.

00:06:48.860 --> 00:06:51.080
When I have a patient who...

00:06:51.080 --> 00:06:52.650
I guess it's not the
same as this situation,

00:06:52.650 --> 00:06:53.910
but let's say I have a patient who doesn't

00:06:53.910 --> 00:06:55.150
have any symptoms,

00:06:55.150 --> 00:06:57.870
and I order blood tests
for a screening purpose,

00:06:57.870 --> 00:07:01.410
and something comes out
horribly inaccurate signaling a

00:07:01.410 --> 00:07:02.630
form of disease,

00:07:02.630 --> 00:07:05.580
my first job is to recheck
that lab value to make sure

00:07:05.580 --> 00:07:07.530
that there wasn't a mistake,

00:07:07.530 --> 00:07:08.560
'cause that does happen!

00:07:08.560 --> 00:07:11.290
There's been times where I
checked the red blood cell count

00:07:11.290 --> 00:07:12.200
of my patient,

00:07:12.200 --> 00:07:14.170
and found them to be horribly anemic.

00:07:14.170 --> 00:07:15.003
It didn't make sense.

00:07:15.003 --> 00:07:16.500
It didn't fit their clinical symptoms.

00:07:16.500 --> 00:07:18.340
It didn't fit their vital signs,

00:07:18.340 --> 00:07:20.560
so I asked the lab to repeat the test,

00:07:20.560 --> 00:07:21.570
and voila!

00:07:21.570 --> 00:07:23.080
Their labs were totally normal.

00:07:23.080 --> 00:07:26.530
So, if you feel that the clinical
presentation doesn't match

00:07:26.530 --> 00:07:28.600
what's on the blood, recheck it.

00:07:28.600 --> 00:07:30.536
- You know there's only
one more person to talk to.

00:07:30.536 --> 00:07:31.530
(power guitar chord)

00:07:31.530 --> 00:07:33.500
- [J.D. Voiceover] The hematopathologist.

00:07:33.500 --> 00:07:35.580
(thunder booms)

00:07:35.580 --> 00:07:36.745
- Hello?

00:07:36.745 --> 00:07:39.670
- Call me Dr. Bob.
- You go by your first name?

00:07:39.670 --> 00:07:40.930
- No, first name's Fred.

00:07:40.930 --> 00:07:42.020
- Fred Bob?

00:07:42.020 --> 00:07:43.412
- What can I do you for?

00:07:43.412 --> 00:07:44.786
- (laughs) I love that interaction!

00:07:44.786 --> 00:07:46.920
- I was hoping you could recheck
Ben Sullivan's blood smear.

00:07:46.920 --> 00:07:48.620
- Well, that depends, young man.

00:07:48.620 --> 00:07:50.280
Do you actually think I made a mistake,

00:07:50.280 --> 00:07:52.280
or do you just wish I did?

00:07:52.280 --> 00:07:54.040
- I kinda wish you did.

00:07:54.040 --> 00:07:54.873
- Then I'll do it.

00:07:54.873 --> 00:07:57.195
- Sorry there, giant,
'70s, security guard guy.

00:07:57.195 --> 00:07:59.140
(chuckles)

00:07:59.140 --> 00:07:59.973
Cold out here, huh?

00:07:59.973 --> 00:08:01.016
- Yes.

00:08:01.016 --> 00:08:01.849
(grunts)

00:08:01.849 --> 00:08:02.682
- Where is his wound?!

00:08:02.682 --> 00:08:04.653
He just a nail go through his right hand!

00:08:04.653 --> 00:08:08.300
♪ Once we take ♪

00:08:08.300 --> 00:08:11.290
♪ To be always workin' ♪

00:08:11.290 --> 00:08:13.560
- Being a lab nerd, that was a mistake.

00:08:13.560 --> 00:08:16.290
Whenever you put a
slide onto a microscope,

00:08:16.290 --> 00:08:20.250
you have to use the lowest
magnification possible so that

00:08:20.250 --> 00:08:22.110
you don't put the high magnification on,

00:08:22.110 --> 00:08:24.450
and then end up breaking the slide.

00:08:24.450 --> 00:08:25.680
- Let's try this again.

00:08:25.680 --> 00:08:27.373
Are those the test results?

00:08:30.530 --> 00:08:31.363
- Uh, yeah.

00:08:35.989 --> 00:08:37.643
Ben, you have leukemia.

00:08:46.180 --> 00:08:47.063
- This sucks.

00:08:51.300 --> 00:08:52.133
- Yeah.

00:08:52.133 --> 00:08:54.170
- As much as they try to
make light of the situation,

00:08:54.170 --> 00:08:56.550
I think the way that
he delivered that news

00:08:56.550 --> 00:08:57.848
is actually ideal.

00:08:57.848 --> 00:08:59.400
When you're straightforward,

00:08:59.400 --> 00:09:01.500
and honest and quick to the point,

00:09:01.500 --> 00:09:04.260
that's the best way of
delivering bad news.

00:09:04.260 --> 00:09:06.810
You have to understand what
a patient is going through.

00:09:06.810 --> 00:09:08.660
You have to give them time to process it.

00:09:08.660 --> 00:09:10.590
If you feel that they need
some more information,

00:09:10.590 --> 00:09:12.330
or reassurance, you can give that to them,

00:09:12.330 --> 00:09:14.400
but I like to hold out and ask them what

00:09:14.400 --> 00:09:15.460
questions they have.

00:09:15.460 --> 00:09:17.910
And understand that when
you're delivering bad news,

00:09:17.910 --> 00:09:19.880
a lot of it is not gonna stick,

00:09:19.880 --> 00:09:21.430
especially the dense information,

00:09:21.430 --> 00:09:23.570
like about treatment options, this, that.

00:09:23.570 --> 00:09:25.740
You have to let the patient process it.

00:09:25.740 --> 00:09:27.720
Just give them a little
piece of information,

00:09:27.720 --> 00:09:28.950
and then just be there for them.

00:09:28.950 --> 00:09:31.490
They can scream, they can
yell, they can be quiet,

00:09:31.490 --> 00:09:32.710
everyone has different reactions,

00:09:32.710 --> 00:09:34.380
depending on their personality,

00:09:34.380 --> 00:09:36.500
and it's not an easy situation.

00:09:36.500 --> 00:09:38.410
This also hits home for me,

00:09:38.410 --> 00:09:39.400
because, unfortunately,

00:09:39.400 --> 00:09:42.710
I lost my mom while in
medical school to leukemia,

00:09:42.710 --> 00:09:44.470
a form of leukemia known as CLL.

00:09:44.470 --> 00:09:45.950
Delivering this type of news is something

00:09:45.950 --> 00:09:47.020
I have to do often.

00:09:47.020 --> 00:09:48.330
It's a tough situation, all around,

00:09:48.330 --> 00:09:50.050
but if you can be straightforward,
you can be honest,

00:09:50.050 --> 00:09:51.440
and really care...

00:09:51.440 --> 00:09:52.273
And even cry!

00:09:52.273 --> 00:09:53.180
That's okay.

00:09:53.180 --> 00:09:55.130
A lot of doctors think they
have to be unemotional,

00:09:55.130 --> 00:09:56.110
and not cry.

00:09:56.110 --> 00:09:57.330
If you feel the, to cry, cry.

00:09:57.330 --> 00:09:58.547
You're human, that's okay.

00:09:58.547 --> 00:10:01.120
"Scrubs" is truly starting to grow on me,

00:10:01.120 --> 00:10:03.390
so please keep recommending the episodes,

00:10:03.390 --> 00:10:06.010
and click on this playlist
that I created of my favorite

00:10:06.010 --> 00:10:07.280
medical drama reviews.

00:10:07.280 --> 00:10:08.863
Stay happy and healthy.

